Tracing the actual code: joinPoolAndStake is invoked via _delegateCall(LP_LIB, ...) so WETH.withdraw(msgValue) and the
  subsequent add_liquidity{value: msgValue} both execute in the vault's own storage/balance context. The only facts
  visible in this file about the ETH leg are (1) Curve's ALT_ETH is rewritten to the internal ETH_ADDRESS sentinel, (2)
  on entry the vault unwraps exactly the ETH-leg amount from WETH to native ETH before the pool call, and (3) on exit
  unstakeAndExitPool re-wraps the ETH-leg exit balance back into WETH. Those two halves are symmetric and encode a
  single stated convention: the vault holds the ETH leg as WETH at rest, and native ETH exists only transiently inside
  the pool call. The receive() on the lib is explicitly documented as existing only for the checkReentrancyContext path,
  which is further evidence that native ETH is not the resting representation.

  The claim rests entirely on _executeDepositTrades in AbstractSingleSidedLP, which is NOT included in the input. I
  cannot see whether it passes ETH_ADDRESS verbatim to the trading module, whether the trading module normalizes ETH to
  WETH on the buy side, whether the abstract layer wraps received native ETH after the trade, or whether the amounts
  array is recomputed from token balances after trading. Every step of the alleged break — "buyToken =
  address(tokens[i]) = ETH_ADDRESS", "the trading module delivers native ETH", "the vault holds no matching WETH" — is
  an assertion about unshown code, not a control-flow fact I can point at. The exit-side re-wrap in the code I can see
  cuts the other way: it shows the codebase deliberately converts native ETH to WETH whenever ETH leaves the pool,
  consistent with _executeDepositTrades also landing WETH.

  Furthermore, the first branch of the alleged impact ("entry permanently unusable on every ETH-legged pool whose asset
  is not the ETH leg") is a deploy-time configuration failure that reverts deterministically and moves no funds — it
  would be caught on the first integration test of such a deployment, and choosing that pool/asset pairing is a
  privileged deployment decision, not an unprivileged attacker action. The second branch ("unrelated WETH is present in
  the vault") presupposes stray WETH sitting in the vault outside LP accounting, which the single-sided-LP accounting
  model (balances measured before/after, as _exitPool does for V2) does not establish exists.

  No unprivileged attacker-controlled step is identified: joinPoolAndStake is reached only through the vault's own
  deposit flow with amounts derived from the depositor's own capital, and the alleged mismatch, if it existed, would
  revert rather than transfer value to the caller.

  Per the tie-break rule — the decisive code (_executeDepositTrades / AbstractSingleSidedLP) is not shown, the path is
  inferred rather than traced, and the visible entry/exit symmetry actively contradicts the premise — this cannot be
  called unambiguously real.

  VERDICT|REFUTED|src/single-sided-lp/CurveConvex2Token.sol:joinPoolAndStake|class=C22|The claim's decisive step
  (_executeDepositTrades delivering native ETH rather than WETH) lives in AbstractSingleSidedLP, which is not in the
  reviewed code, while the visible entry-unwrap/exit-rewrap symmetry (WETH.withdraw on join, WETH.deposit{value:
  exitBalances[i]} on exit) establishes the opposite convention that the vault holds the ETH leg as WETH at rest, and
  the alleged failure is a deterministic revert under a privileged deployment configuration rather than an unprivileged
  value-extracting exploit.
